# DESCRIPTION

Processes talk in two tiers.
A channel carries small messages and handles, copied by the kernel: it is the control plane, for bootstrap, for passing capabilities and for rare requests.
A ring is memory both sides map, holding two queues and two arenas: it is the data plane, for everything with volume, and when both sides are busy the kernel is not on its path at all.
A server normally hands out a ring through a channel, and the client then speaks its protocol, 9Px most often, over the ring.

```text
             control plane                          data plane
client -- channel (copied, <= 64 KiB, <= 64 handles) --> server
client == ring (one shared VMO; no kernel on the path) == server
            doorbell: a counter; the wake-up lands on a port
```

Every wait is on a port.
A thread binds what it cares about, a channel end, a ring end's doorbell, a counter, a task's exit, an interrupt, to one port, and makes one call, `port_wait`, with a deadline as its argument; there is no timer object.
Locks, which are held briefly, wait on a futex instead.
time(7) says more of deadlines and the one clock.

The kernel provides the objects and checks the rights on their handles (see handles(7)); the protocols are user space's.
`lib/vx-ring` keeps a ring's queues, `lib/vx-ring/session.c` opens one through a channel, and `lib/vx-9p` speaks 9Px over one, client and server.

@node=channels title="Channels" keys=message,datagram,txid,call,reply,listen,post

A channel, made by channel_create(2), is a pair of ends.
What is written to one end is queued at the other, as a datagram, not part of a stream: a body of 16 bytes to 64 KiB, which starts with a 16-byte header, and up to 64 handles.
The header's <txid> matches a reply to its call, <ordinal> is the protocol's operation, <flags> is the protocol's, and <sender_intent> is written by the kernel, so a reader can trust it.
The body is copied twice, into the kernel and out; the handles move, leaving the writer's table with the rights they had.

Each end's queue holds at most 64 messages and 1 MiB, and a write to a full one fails with `VX_ERR_SHOULD_WAIT`: a flood fails its sender and never grows the kernel.
Reading never blocks; a reader binds its end to a port with `VX_TRIGGER_READABLE`, and with `VX_TRIGGER_PEER_CLOSED` to hear that the other end has gone.

`channel_call` writes a request and waits for its reply in one call.
The kernel picks the request's <txid>, and a reply with that <txid> goes straight to the waiting caller, never through the queue.
It is how a client asks a server for a ring, and how small requests are made, such as bus-acpi(8)'s to devmgr(8) for a region of a device's memory, answered with its handle.

A service's channel is its post.
svcd(8) makes a channel for each `post=` in a manifest (see svc(6)), keeps the client end as `/srv/<name>`, and gives the server end to the service as its listen channel; a client is given a duplicate of the client end, its connector, and a mount names a post.
svcd keeps a second handle to the server end, so a connection asked for while a service restarts waits for it.
services(7) describes posts and manifests.

@node=waiting title="Ports, counters and futexes" keys=wait,binding,trigger,packet,deadline,leeway,counter,futex

A port, port_create(2), is a queue of 32-byte packets, each with a key chosen by whoever bound or posted it, a value, a timestamp on the monotonic clock, the source's handle and the trigger.
A binding, `port_bind`, is one-shot: when its trigger holds on its source, the port gets one packet and the binding is gone.
If the trigger already holds when the call is made the packet comes at once, so no event is lost between looking at a source and binding it; a program re-binds after handling each packet.
Each binding is allocated as the packet it will deliver, so bindings never overflow a port.
`port_wait` returns up to 64 packets at once, and `port_post` queues packets of a program's own, to wake itself.

A counter, counter_create(2), holds a 64-bit value that only goes up.
`counter_signal` raises it, and a `VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE` binding fires when it reaches the binding's threshold.
It is a timeline: a producer raises it as work completes, and any holder of a handle waits for a value.
Each ring end has one as its doorbell, which `counter_read` reads and `port_bind` waits on through the end itself.

A futex, futex_wait(2), is a 32-bit word in memory a thread sleeps on while it holds a value it saw.
It is keyed by the word's physical address, so a word in a VMO two tasks map is one futex for both (see sharing(7)).
It is for locks and condition variables; anything waited on for long goes through a port.

@node=layout title="A ring's memory" keys=header,vxrb,index,line,entry,sqe,cqe,arena,layout

ring_create(2) makes a ring from its parameters: the lengths of the submission queue (SQ) and the completion queue (CQ), powers of two up to 4096; the size of their entries, multiples of 16 up to 256 bytes; and the size of each side's arena.
It returns three handles: the client's end, the server's end, and the ring's memory, one VMO, which each side maps with as_map(2) wherever it likes.
The kernel writes the header into the VMO and never reads the ring again.

A ring as 9Px uses it, with 8 entries in each queue, `vx_sqe` and `vx_cqe` entries, a 32 KiB client arena and a 48 KiB server arena, is laid out so, in bytes from the start of the VMO:

```text
     0  header: magic "VXRB", version 1, queue lengths, entry sizes,
        features, offsets and sizes of the queues and arenas, size
  4096  SQ tail     written by the client     each index line has a
  4160  SQ head     written by the server     64-byte cache line of
          and its NEED_WAKEUP flag            its own, so no two sides
  4224  CQ tail     written by the server     write one line
  4288  CQ head     written by the client
          and its NEED_WAKEUP flag
  8192  SQ: 8 entries of 64 bytes (vx_sqe), the client's requests
  8704  CQ: 8 entries of 32 bytes (vx_cqe), the server's completions
 12288  client arena, 32 KiB: written by the client, read by the server
 45056  server arena, 48 KiB: written by the server, read by the client
 94208  end
```

The queues start a page past the index lines, the CQ on the next 64-byte boundary after the SQ, and the arenas on page boundaries; `vx_ring_layout` in `lib/vx-ring/ring.c` computes the offsets, the kernel and both sides alike.

The generic entries are in `abi/vx/abi.h`.
A submission, `vx_sqe`, is one 64-byte line: an <opcode>, <flags> (`VX_SQE_DREF`, data in the arena; `VX_SQE_HANDLES`, handles in a slot; `VX_SQE_LINK` and `VX_SQE_FENCE`, which no protocol uses yet), a <user_data> echoed in the completion, a <target> and an <offset> that the protocol defines, an arena range <arena_off> and <len>, a <handle_slot>, and 16 bytes inline.
A completion, `vx_cqe`, is 32 bytes: the <user_data>, a <result>, and <flags>, <aux> and <aux2> that the protocol defines.
An entry has no priority of its own: a ring's service class is meant to be the ring's, fixed when it is made.

Each side writes only its own index lines and its own arena, and allocates in its arena as it likes: there is no shared allocator.
A buffer in an arena is free again when the peer's reply has said it is done with it.
Payloads too large or too long-lived for an arena go in VMOs of their own, sent as handles (see sharing(7)).

@node=protocol title="Producing, consuming and sleeping" keys=spsc,wait-free,release,acquire,fence,need_wakeup,doorbell,notify,hostile,handles,slot

Each queue has one producer and one consumer: the client produces submissions and consumes completions, the server the reverse.
So both are wait-free, and one client cannot corrupt another's queue, since each client has a ring of its own.
Indices run free and wrap at 2^32, masked by the queue's length less one.

A producer writes an entry, stores the new tail with release order, then, after a full fence, looks at the consumer's `NEED_WAKEUP` flag; only if it is set does it ring the consumer's doorbell with ring_notify(2).
A consumer loads the tail with acquire order, copies out each entry up to it, and stores its head with release order.
When it finds nothing, it reads its own doorbell, sets its flag, fences, and looks at the tail once more: only if the queue is still empty does it bind its end to its port with `VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE` and a threshold one past the doorbell it read, and wait.
One of the two fences always sees the other side's store, so a wake-up is never lost, and while both sides are busy neither enters the kernel.
`vx-check`, an exhaustive checker of interleavings, explores this protocol, and four broken variants of it that it must reject, in `tests/host/ring_model_test.c`.

`lib/vx-ring` has these as `vx_ring_produce_slot` and `vx_ring_produce`, which says whether to ring; `vx_ring_consume`; and `vx_ring_prepare_sleep` and `vx_ring_end_sleep` around the wait.

Shared memory is hostile, since whoever made the ring, or the peer, may be.
A side attaches with `vx_ring_attach`, which takes the header only if it is exactly what its own protocol's parameters make, and keeps a private copy; the header in shared memory is never read again.
Each entry is copied out exactly once and then checked, never read twice.
An index that runs ahead of the other, more entries ready than the queue holds, marks the ring broken, and the side treats the peer as gone; an arena range outside the peer's arena does the same in vx-9p.

An entry cannot carry a handle, since the kernel never reads the ring.
A side moves up to 4 handles into one of 16 slots the kernel keeps for its direction, with ring_xfer_handles(2), and names the slot in the entry; the peer takes them from the slot as it handles the entry.
Handles left in slots go with the ring.

@node=sessions title="Opening a ring" keys=connect,dial,accept,session,listen,connector

A ring is opened through a channel.
The client sends a request with `channel_call` to its connector; the server, reading its listen channel, makes a ring with its protocol's parameters, maps it, keeps the server end, and replies with two handles, the client end and the ring's memory.
The client maps the memory, attaches, and closes the memory's handle, which the mapping keeps.
A refusal is a reply with no handles, its <flags> a status when the server gives one.

So every connection is its own ring, as one producer per queue requires, and a connection is never shared: a child that inherits a mount connects again with its copy of the connector, and `fork` copies no ring's memory into the child.
`lib/vx-ring/session.c` has this as `vx_session_dial` for the client and `vx_session_accept` for the server, with a version that asks without waiting, `vx_session_ask` and `vx_session_answer`, for a client that cannot stall on a server not yet there, as netd(4) waits for its network driver.
9Px makes the same exchange, with its own ordinal, in `lib/vx-9p`.

@node=9px title="9Px on a ring, and over TCP" keys=9p,9px,msize,transport,tcp,dial,9px+shm

On a ring, the local transport, each 9Px message is one submission: opcode `P9_RING_MSG`, its bytes in the client's arena at <arena_off>, <len> long.
Its reply is one completion: <result> is the reply's length, and <aux2> its offset in the server's arena.
The connection request is the ordinal `P9_CONNECT`, "cnp9", on the server's listen channel.
The ring has 8 entries each way, and a message is at most 16384 bytes, the msize version(5) negotiates on a ring.

Up to 8 requests are in flight on a connection, and the server answers them in any order (M6 step 6d4a); the threads of a process share a connection.
Each side knows what of its arena is free without asking:
- a request's bytes stay in the client's arena until its reply comes (or the `Rflush` that ends it), as the server may read them again while it holds the request, so the client takes the arena in 512-byte chunks and gives them back in any order;
- a reply's bytes are freed as the client consumes the completion, which it does in order, so the server places replies in order.
Each request reserves room for the largest reply it can have, a read's count, an msize for a stat, 512 bytes for the rest, and the reservations stay an msize short of the server's arena, so the replies a server holds always fit; a client past that is broken, and the server drops it.
One of the 8 slots is kept for `Tflush`.
A server holds a request it cannot do yet, as many as 8, and goes on taking more; one on a fid an earlier held request is on waits behind it, so a pipe's writes keep their order (flush(5) has `Tflush`).

A VMO travels beside a message in a handle slot: `Tmap`'s reply carries the file's VMO, with `P9_CQE_HANDLE` in the completion's <flags> and the slot in <aux> (see map(5)), and `Treadref` and `Twriteref` carry the client's, with `VX_SQE_HANDLES` and <handle_slot> (see dref(5)).
These, and the server-kept open files of the `posix` extension, are why the client offers 9Px's extensions only on a ring.

Over a network a connection is TCP.
A mount of `tcp!`<host>`!`<port> asks the namespace's `/net/cs` where to dial, connects a conversation in `/net/tcp`, and speaks 9P over its data file, each message framed by its size as on any 9P stream: netd(4) serves the conversation, over a ring of its own.
The client offers `9P2000.x/1` with no extensions, which a Plan 9 server answers with `9P2000`, and asks for an msize of 16384.
Each process dials its own connection, since a child cannot be handed one as it is handed a connector.
vx9pserve(8) is a host's server for such mounts; namespace(7) describes mounts.

@node=flow title="Flow control" keys=backpressure,credit,budget,queue,full,defer,held

Nothing in the system grows without bound because a peer is fast.
A channel's queue is bounded, and a full one fails its writer.
A ring's queue is bounded by its length, and its depth is the client's credit: a client can have no more requests outstanding than the queue holds, and a server can complete no more than the client has asked for.
A port's posts are bounded at 64, and its bindings cannot overflow it.

vx-9p's server keeps to these.
It serves at most 8 requests from one connection before it turns to the next, so a client that keeps its queue full gets its share and not the whole server.
A request it cannot answer yet, such as a read of a terminal with nothing typed, is held, and its connection takes nothing more until it is answered; every other connection is served meanwhile.
A client that leaves its completion queue full, so a reply has nowhere to go, is disconnected, as is one that breaks the protocol.
A server holds 16 connections unless it gives itself more.

vx-9p's client has one request in flight on a connection, at offset 0 of its arena, and waits for its reply.
The block protocol (`docs/proto/block.md`) is the one that keeps many in flight today: 128 entries each way, completions in any order, and a 1 MiB client arena that the disk's driver gives its device by DMA, so the device reads and writes the client's buffers directly (see sharing(7)).

@node=server title="A server's loop" keys=loop,serve,drain,arm,sleep,port_wait,key,generation

A server is one loop around one port, as `p9_ring_serve` in `lib/vx-9p/ring_server.c` is:

- Drain each connection: consume its submissions, up to its budget, copy each request out of the client's arena, serve it, and produce its completion, ringing the client's doorbell if the client sleeps.
- Read the listen channel until it is empty, answering each connection request with a new ring.
- If nothing is left to do, prepare to sleep on each connection, which sets its `NEED_WAKEUP` and looks once more; bind each idle connection's doorbell, and the listen channel, to the port where they are not bound already.
- Wait on the port, with a deadline if the server has timers of its own, and handle what comes: a doorbell, a client gone (`VX_TRIGGER_PEER_CLOSED` on its end), the listen channel, or a source of the server's own, such as a driver's interrupt.
- End the sleep on every connection, and go round again.

A packet's key names a connection by its slot and the slot's generation, so a packet from a binding on a connection that has gone is not taken for the slot's next connection.
A server that works so needs one thread, and the file servers built on vx-9p have one.

# EXAMPLES

vx-9p's client waiting for its reply (`lib/vx-9p/ring.c`, `p9_ring_rpc`, lines rewrapped): the doorbell is read before the sleep is announced, and the wait ends early if the server has gone.

```c
for (;;) {
  int64_t n = p9_ring_take(k, resp, cap);
  if (n > 0) return (size_t)n;
  if (n < 0) break;
  int64_t seen = vx_counter_read(k->end);
  if (vx_ring_prepare_sleep(&k->ring)) {
    vx_packet pk = {};
    vx_port_bind(k->port, k->end, VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE, P9_KEY_BELL,
                 (uint64_t)seen + 1);
    int64_t got = vx_port_wait(k->port, deadline, 0, &pk, 1);
    if (got == VX_ERR_INTERRUPTED) {
      vx_ring_end_sleep(&k->ring);
      continue;
    }
    if (got != 1 || pk.key == P9_KEY_CLOSED) break;
  }
  vx_ring_end_sleep(&k->ring);
}
```

The `P9_KEY_CLOSED` packet comes from a `VX_TRIGGER_PEER_CLOSED` binding made once, when the connection was opened.

