# BUGS

`channel_call` does not yet switch straight to a waiting server on the caller's scheduling context, nor lend handles for the length of a call; every message's <sender_intent> is `VX_INTENT_INTERACTIVE`, and `ring_create` takes no service class, so a ring has none (01 §4.5, §4.3).
Scheduling contexts are not built yet; leases lent for one call come in M6 step 6e.

A counter's value is not yet in a read-only shared page, so every doorbell read before a sleep is a system call.
A consumer never spins before it sleeps, as the design lets a real-time one.

vx-9p's client has one request in flight, and every reply is at arena offset 0: pipelining, and `Tflush` with it, come in M6 step 6d, so a read a server holds waits until it is answered.
Each process dials its own TCP connection for a `tcp!` mount (sharing one through a post comes with 6d), and TCP connections have no Noise handshake: 9Px over TCP is plain 9P2000 with no authentication.

Handles are lost, not left in place, when all 16 of a direction's slots are full or a take is too small; a reply too large for a call's buffers is thrown away (see ring(2) and channel(2)); both are to be fixed with 6d.
`port_cancel` does nothing, and bindings are made one at a time, not in the design's batches.

A server's 16 connections have no per-client limit, so one client can take them all; that comes before M10.
Channel queues, port posts and rings are not charged to any budget.

Both sides map the whole ring writable, the header and the peer's lines and arena included, where the design makes the header read-only after creation; each side reads only its validated copy of the header, so a peer that writes there changes nothing but its own connection.

A ring is one VMO, and a VMO is at most 256 MiB, so `ring_create` refuses arenas larger in all than that, though it takes each up to 1 GiB.

Pipes between POSIX processes are channels of 4 KiB messages, not rings (see posix(7)).
